Nobody edits files inside their site-packages. The registry already recorded
WHICH catalogue families are user-extensible and what each one's overlay
directory is called (ruling 7); what it could not say is where on disk that
directory lives, because there was nowhere.

`hypergumbo init-catalogs` creates $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypergumbo/ with one
<family>.d/ per user-extensible family, a README explaining the layout, and the
shipped community overlays seeded into io_primitives.d/. The repo tier is
<repo>/.hypergumbo/ -- a DIRECTORY, one character and one kind away from
ADR-0045's .hypergumbo.toml FILE, and pinned distinct by a test.

SEED, NEVER COPY (ruling 2), which is the property whose violation is silent.
Base catalogues are never materialized; only deltas land in the user's
directory, because a full copy means the next release's rows never reach that
user and nothing says so. The gate is a test asserting the seeded filenames and
the shipped catalogue filenames are disjoint SETS, not a sentence in a
docstring. Each seeded file is stamped `seeded_from:` beside its existing
`retrieved:` date (ruling 5), and re-running never overwrites a file the user
edited -- it reports it as kept, because a command that eats your edits is a
command you run once.

EXPLICIT, NEVER IMPLICIT (ruling 4), asserted as something that does NOT
happen. Analysis never writes here: the community overlays already load from
the wheel, so materialization buys the ability to EDIT them and nothing else.
ADR-0045's precedent is a human-owned config file the tool may read and must
not write, and a test pins that loading catalogues leaves the home absent.

THE DIRECTORY LIST IS DERIVED FROM YAML_CATALOGS, not written down a second
time. A family that gains a user_channel gains a directory with no edit in the
new module, and a family carrying no_channel_reason cannot acquire one by being
overlooked.

AND THE HOME IS WIRED, BECAUSE OTHERWISE IT IS DECORATION. Nothing read
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypergumbo/io_primitives.d/ -- the ADR-0045 `io_primitives`
setting is a list of paths a user NAMES, not a scan. So hypergumbo has been
printing "Override them in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypergumbo/io_primitives.d/" on
every run that loads a community overlay (the ruling-6 disclosure) while
reading nothing there. Measured before the fix: a row dropped in that directory
changed no boundary. Seeding files into an unread directory would have turned
one false instruction into a much larger one, so the scan is wired here, at the
BOTTOM of the precedence chain -- a path NAMED in config.toml is a more
specific statement of intent than a file dropped in a directory. Verified end
to end: the same probe now reports the row.

The scan leans on INV-lufib rather than inventing a second filter. The seeded
directory holds overlays for five languages at once, so every one reaches every
language's load; INV-lufib already ruled that an overlay for another SHIPPED
language is "not mine" and is skipped, while one naming a language no catalogue
knows is a typo and still raises. A test materializes the real home and asserts
a python load survives it.

THE README SAYS WHICH CHANNELS ARE LIVE. io_primitives.d is scanned; the other
five are declared extensible and not yet consulted, and the README written into
the home says so per family rather than leaving a user to find out through an
overlay that silently does nothing. Wiring the rest is WI-sofov.

TWO DEFECTS FOUND BY THE TESTS RATHER THAN BY REVIEW.

  1. The seeded files were being CORRUPTED. An early draft stamped the version
     with `!r` and a trailing .replace("'", '"') to get YAML double quotes.
     Implicit string concatenation binds BEFORE the method call, so the replace
     ran over the ENTIRE file and turned every apostrophe in the source
     comments into a quote -- shipping mangled prose into a user's config home.
     The comment-preservation test caught it; the version is now rendered with
     json.dumps, and the docstring records why.

  2. The did-you-mean guard's subcommand list was a SECOND HOME for a fact the
     parser already owned. `init-catalogs` parsed correctly and was then
     refused as "not a valid subcommand" with a suggestion to run `catalog`
     instead. It is now derived from the parser, so a registered command cannot
     be unlisted. The literal held EXACTLY the derived set -- checked before
     the swap -- so this changed no behaviour, and a test pins the two in
     agreement.

SCOPE, STATED PLAINLY: `repo_catalogue_home()` NAMES the repo tier's location
and nothing loads it yet. Ruling 9 keeps that tier opt-in and forbids
hypergumbo from ever writing into an analysed repository; wiring the opt-in is
WI-putat's, not this change's.

The subcommand NAME is mine: ADR-0047 specifies "an explicit subcommand" and
never names one. `init-catalogs` follows the existing verb-first
build-grammars / install-embeddings shape. Reversible.

Two existing gates moved with the change: the docs-prose gate that parsed
the subcommand LITERAL out of main() is re-aimed to assert the DERIVATION
is still in place (reintroducing a literal restores the hazard silently),
and .ci/cli-flag-matrix.json is regenerated through its own
HYPERGUMBO_UPDATE_CLI_MATRIX path -- the diff is exactly this subcommand's
four flags.

Coverage: catalogue_home.py 100%, and every new line of the CLI block covered.
